1622		field identifications are understood. Thus each extended point projects
1623		onto its two source lines, with affine scalar coordinates $\alpha(t)$
1624		and $\beta(t)$ as in Definition~\ref{def:combine-map}. An axis-parallel
1625		extended line has axis-parallel source lines.
1626	
1627		Finally there is one probability law on
1628		$(\mathrm v,i,j)\in\{\mathrm{ALine},\mathrm{DLine}\}
1629		\times\{0,\dots,m-1\}^2$ with the following two mixture properties.
1630		Sample a triple from $D_{\mathrm{dir}}$ and an independent uniform
1631		parameter $t$ on $\ell$. The law of $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,\pi_X(\ell(t)))$
1632		is the mixture obtained by sampling $(\ell_X,x)$ from
1633		$D_{\mathrm v,i}$ and independently $\ell_Z$ from the line marginal of
1634		$D_{\mathrm v,j}$, and retaining $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,x)$.
1635		The law of $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,\pi_Z(\ell(t)))$ is the mixture obtained
1636		by retaining $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,z)$ from the corresponding independent
1637		samples of $\ell_X$ and $(\ell_Z,z)$. These are separate one-point
1638		marginal identities, with the same mixture weights; they impose no
1639		joint conditional independence of the two projected points.
1640		Identification with the source law $D$ is a separate obligation;
1641		see \cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.
